{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 60,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Spoiler alert: this won't end well.\nThere are five large worlds that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5940,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "share names with chemical elements: the \nplanets Mercury, Uranus, and Neptune,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9780,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the dwarf planets Ceres and Pluto. What \nif - 2 have some fun - each world suddenly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14820,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "became composed of its corresponding element?\nMercury and cerium are metals, so Mercury  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20100,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Ceres would mostly just get slightly \nheavier and shinier. From earth they'd look  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24240,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a bit brighter in the night sky, enough that \nCeres would become visible to the naked eye. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28140,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unfortunately, the night sky - and human \neyes - would get a little harder to find,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32580,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thanks to the other planets.\nPlutonium, uranium and neptunium  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36420,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are radioactive; plutonium and uranium do have \n(non-fissile) isotopes which decay slowly and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42000,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mainly produce a bit of heat. A small lump \nof Uranium’s most common and stable isotope  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46260,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wouldn’t even be hot to the touch. But if you \ncollected it into a planet-size ball, the tiny  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50520,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of heat produced by each part would add up \nto heat up the planet to thousands of degrees.* "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54240,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It might seem strange that something that's \ncool in small amounts would be hot when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58200,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collected together in a big ball, but this is \njust a consequence of geometry and the physics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62340,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of radiating heat. Since volume grows faster than \nsurface area, and the volume is where the heat is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67260,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produced, the interiors of large heat-producing \nobjects produce more heat relative to their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71640,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface areas. But thermodynamics doesn't allow \nobjects to just radiate more heat - they have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76500,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get hotter to do so; the hotter they are, the \nmore heat they're allowed to radiate. So a large  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81600,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heat-producing object will produce more heat \nthan it can radiate away until that heat builds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85800,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up (enough) and the object gets hot - hot enough \nthat it can radiate away enough heat. Really big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90120,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects can get extremely hot from just a tiny \namount of heat production per unit of volume. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94560,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like, the Sun. A cup of the sun's core * produces \nabout 60 milliwatts of thermal energy. By volume,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100200,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s about the same heat production rate as \nthe body of a lizard, and substantially less  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103920,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than that of a human. In a sense, you are hotter \nthan the Sun—there’s just not as much of you. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108600,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we were talking about Uranus, which there \nis a lot of, and which would get really really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113040,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hot if made from uranium. The real Uranus, \nlit by the sun, is too dim to see with the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117900,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "naked eye. But the superhot uranium Uranus \nwould glow bright enough to be visible like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121740,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an ordinary star in the night sky.\nAnd plutonium Pluto would heat up and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125940,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "glow enough that from earth it would also be \nvisible to the naked eye - though just barely. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129660,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except you wouldn't be spending much \ntime looking at the night sky anymore,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132780,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thanks to neptunium Neptune.\nEven the most stable neptunium isotope  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136680,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is fissile, so 237Neptune would instantly \nundergo a runaway fission chain reaction,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140580,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "converting the planet into an expanding cloud \nof high-energy particles and X-rays. Around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145200,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "four hours later, the shock wave would reach—and \ncompletely obliterate—the Earth, stripping away  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149820,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its surface and everything on it and leaving \nbehind a molten blob. We'd have gotten similar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "results for Uranus and Pluto if we'd instead used \nfissile isotopes of uranium or plutonium, though  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158640,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a bonus Uranus' shock wave would reach (and \ndestroy) us about an hour faster than Neptune's. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163740,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's a simple takeaway from all this: If you \nhave a choice between isotopes and you’re not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168120,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sure which to pick, go for the most stable one. \nAnd just stay away from neptunium altogether. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173460,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok - I'll avoid neptunium. But what if to be \nsilly we filled the solar system with soup  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178380,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out to Jupiter? Or what if to pass the time \nwe spun the earth up so that a day lasted a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183120,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "second? Or what if to avoid getting in trouble \nyou tried to read every single law that applies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to you? What if to answer these questions \nyou could just read a book called What if 2,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191640,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that this video is based on and supported by? \nWhat if two reasons to read what if 2 are that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196800,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was written by Randall Munroe and that it has \nover 60 answers to important \"what if\" questions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201240,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like \"what if Japan disappeared?\" What if to read \nwhat if 2 you just had to look at the information  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206400,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the description of this video? What if two \nbe clear, is available wherever books are sold,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212040,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and when you get yourself a copy, \nthen you can ask \"what if,\" too."
    } ]
  } ]
}
